TY  - BOOK
TI  - Freedom within Reason
AU  - Wolf, Susan
PY  - 1990
DO  - 10.1093/oso/9780195056167.001.0001
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1093/oso/9780195056167.001.0001
AB  - In this work, the author argues that, contrary to the tradition in analytic philosophy, freedom of the will cannot be understood as a purely metaphysical notion. You enjoy freedom of the will only if you are in a position that enables you, not only to govern your actions on the basis of your own values, but also to develop and alter your values on the basis of the right data and by the right means. Accordingly, free will is the freedom to be good, or the freedom to do the right thing for the right reasons. This approach allows us to explain the failure of attempts to provide satisfactory metaphysical accounts of free will, whether compatibilist or in-compatilist: the metaphysical conditions of freedom of the will vary in accordance with the value of the action in question.
